{
  "note": "Re-judged 2026-11-02 by an independent auditor who authored none of 06b26570b6, e145f7bf39 or 0a030d3e7a. RFC 2545 Section 3 read: \"A BGP speaker shall advertise to its peer in the Network Address of Next Hop field the global IPv6 address of the next hop, potentially followed by the link-local IPv6 address of the next hop.\" Units moved since the last verdict: TestFamilyNextHopOriginationAdmission and TestStaticOriginateContinuesAfterUnusablePolicyNextHop changed only the egressRouteFilter stub signature (ff89fc8110 added a third follow-body return; the stubs return nil there, so suppress and override are what they were); every input and assertion is byte-identical. TestRFC2545EffectivePairPolicyAndMixedSibling (06b26570b6) now also installs the in-process rewrite in egressFilters, because the route-server fast path runs those filters and the export chain itself; rfc2545DispatchReceived accepts either a fast-path decision or the hand-back of a by-section withdrawal to the cached rail, and every wire assertion after it is unchanged: exact native next-hop bytes for a valid effective pair (with the off-link trim to 16), withdrawal of an invalid effective pair after policy, the exact section count and an unchanged legacy sibling. The rs rail thus now proves the fast path's own post-chain next-hop validation, which is the new producer. Both clauses (global first; link-local only as the potential second address) stay covered by these and the byte-identical carriers judged before. Ran the changed units at HEAD in a clean worktree under ./le job run: PASS. Separately, ./le rfc check reports this row's revert records over TestFamilyNextHopOriginationAdmission and TestDefaultOriginateAppendsLinkLocalWhenSection3Holds as no longer verifying; they need re-recording with ./le rfc record, which does not change this judgement of the tests' content. Verdict unchanged: enforced.",
